 ## Overview
-Light Propagation Volume (LPV), specifically the Opacity Propagation Volume (OPV) variant, is a real-time method for precomputing the optical depth $T(S,X)$ from each light source $S$ to each cell $X$ in a 3D grid [S1]. It solves the problem of efficiently calculating light attenuation (extinction) through heterogeneous participating media by propagating density information radially from light sources through a 3D volume on the GPU [S1]. Unlike standard radiance propagation which suffers from rapid scattering errors in dense media, OPV focuses on accurate optical depth integration, enabling correct shadowing and attenuation for subsequent ray-marched rendering [S1]. It builds on [Ray-marched Volumetric Lighting with Density Thresholding](2456-ray-marched-volumetric-lighting-with-density-thresholding.md) by providing the precomputed attenuation data required for that rendering step.
+Light Propagation Volume (LPV), specifically the Opacity Propagation Volume (OPV) variant, is a real-time method for precomputing the optical depth $T(S,X)$ from each light source $S$ to each cell $X$ in a 3D grid [S1]. It solves the problem of efficiently calculating light attenuation through heterogeneous participating media by propagating density information radially from light sources through a 3D volume on the GPU [S1]. Unlike standard radiance propagation which suffers from rapid scattering errors in dense media, OPV focuses on accurate optical depth integration, enabling correct shadowing and attenuation for subsequent ray-marched rendering [S1]. It builds on [Ray-marched Volumetric Lighting with Density Thresholding](2456-ray-marched-volumetric-lighting-with-density-thresholding.md) by providing the precomputed attenuation data required for that rendering step [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** A set of light sources $S$, each with a local extinction coefficient. A medium density model defined as a sum of Radial Basis Functions (RBFs).
-*   **Output:** An Opacity Propagation Volume (OPV) storing the optical depth integral $T(X,S)$ for each cell $X$ and light source $S$.
+*   **Input:** A set of light sources $S$, each with a local extinction coefficient. A medium density model defined as a sum of Radial Basis Functions (RBFs) [S1].
+*   **Output:** An Opacity Propagation Volume (OPV) storing the optical depth integral $T(X,S)$ for each cell $X$ and light source $S$ [S1].
 
